Replicate Example 1.31 using
• Average number of rooms in Boston housing data (variable rm);
• Petal length of setosa flowers in iris data.
See Figure 1.21 for the respective histograms.
In each case
Compute the sample quantiles using 2.5 and 97.5 percentiles;
Compute the sample mean $\mu$ and standard deviation $s$;
Compute the theoretical CI using (1.6.1).
How close are the theoretical CI to the ones, based on the sample quantiles?
Answers on page 497

function growTree((X, y))
    let N = number of cases in X
    if $y_i = 0$ for all $i = 1,2,\ldots,N$:
        return leaf(0)
    if $y_i = 1$ for all $i = 1,2,\ldots,N$:
        return leaf(1)
    let j = bestAttribute((X, y))
        # the best way to split (X, y) into two parts
        # one corresponding to $x_j = 0$, the other to $x_j = 1$
    let D = X\_{-j}
        # remove feature j from data
    return node(j, growTree((D\_i, y\_i) for i : x\_{ij} = 0),
        growTree((D\_i, y\_i) for i : x\_{ij} = 1))
